id: decisions/043-automation-cadence
title: "Which bots run on a clock"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'automation-cadence'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: automation-cadence
axis_gloss: "whether a loop runs on a clock whether or not anything happened, or only when an event or a person triggers it"
poles:
  a: on-demand-triggered
  b: scheduled-recurring
split: leaning
draft_status: full
leverage: 5.9
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(7) x split_weight(0.7) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  Should a bot run on a clock or only when something arrives or someone asks,
  and does the answer depend on whether the bot can act outside the repo?

why_it_matters: >
  The schedules already encode a tier: loops that watch inbound surfaces poll
  hourly, loops that produce prose run once or twice a day in a fixed morning
  order, and the one loop that can spend or publish is never scheduled, which a
  test enforces; no file says any of this is intended, and the human side
  (core/human-use.yawn) is entered only when something is felt. A new bot
  inherits a cadence tier or picks one at random; a child Yawn learns whether a
  clock is a trigger or a default.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    5 judgments sit at on-demand-triggered (1 authored) and 2 at
    scheduled-recurring (0 authored). The repository asserts both directions on
    this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether the twice-daily AI-writing scan (10:30 and 16:30) reflects a priority
    Dave holds over license and comprehension risk, or is an accident of when it
    was written.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      Bots run on triggers. A loop runs when an inbound event, a changed head SHA,
      or a named review condition fires; no loop runs on a clock unless the clock is
      written as a trigger with a stated reason.
    cost: "Rewriting eight schedule: lines as trigger conditions; the three hourly polls become event subscriptions or keep a clock with a reason."
    risk: "Polling surfaces (GitHub feedback, the main-branch diff) offer no push event today; a trigger-only rule leaves them unwatched."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "A test over automation/*.yawn asserting any schedule: value is paired with a schedule_reason: that names the surface being watched."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0015"
      - "J0265"
      - "J0334"
      - "J0622"
      - "J0788"
  - name: B
    description: >
      Bots run on schedules. Each contract sets its own cadence; unscheduled loops
      are the exception and say why.
    cost: "One sentence in agents/yawn.bot.policy.yawn; a reason on statement-image-proposals (already present)."
    risk: "A loop that gains a spend or publish effect keeps its schedule; nothing ties cadence to capability."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "None beyond the existing test 'the recurring contract can propose but cannot generate, spend, or publish'."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0033"
      - "J1162"
  - name: C
    description: >
      Cadence follows capability. A loop that watches an inbound surface (feedback,
      PRs, the public branch) polls on a written cadence with a reason. A loop that
      only produces prose runs at most daily. A loop that can spend, publish, send,
      or leave the machine is never scheduled and runs only on a human trigger under
      a one-time approval. Human use of the protocol is never scheduled. Every
      schedule: carries a schedule_reason:.
    cost: "One paragraph in agents/yawn.bot.policy.yawn; a schedule_reason line on eight automation contracts; a decision on whether the twice-daily AI-writing scan stays twice-daily."
    risk: "The tiers need a capability field a bot cannot set for itself; if allowed_effects is bot-authored the tier is bot-chosen."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend tests/interception-statement-image-governance.test.mjs to scan every automation/*.yawn: any contract whose allowed_effects include spend, publish, send or external must have schedule: not-scheduled; any schedule: must be paired with schedule_reason:; prose-only loops may not be more frequent than daily unless the reason says why."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0015"
      - "J0033"
      - "J0265"
      - "J0788"
      - "J1162"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.8
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    GROUND_TRUTH FACT 17 reads the schedule values as a priority order and finds
    the one test-enforced piece: the only spend-capable loop is the only
    unscheduled one ('approval is one-time conditional preauthorization, never
    recurring authority'). J1162 (outcome-attached) and J0033 read the same
    tiering off the other schedules; J0265 and J0788 (on-demand) are the human and
    root-record side, which C keeps unscheduled. The only authored judgment
    (J0015) is a dedupe rule that works under any cadence. C is what the artifacts
    do; it lacks a sentence and a reason field. A would force event subscriptions
    the surfaces do not offer; B would let capability and cadence drift apart.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "agents/yawn.bot.policy.yawn states the capability tiers and that spend, publish, send or external effects are never scheduled"
    - "Every automation/*.yawn with a schedule: carries a schedule_reason:, asserted by a test in tests/ that passes"
    - "The morning order (07:30 radar, 08:20 readme, 08:40 license, 09:00 daily) is either stated as intended or declared arbitrary in one of those reasons"

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    An automation gains an email, publish or spend effect and acquires a schedule
    without a new approval record, and the governance test stays green.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J0015"
  - "J0265"
  - "J0334"
  - "J0622"
  - "J0788"
  - "J0033"
  - "J1162"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
